@@ -2,7 +2,6 @@
 
 This project is a sample news application built with Astro, designed to serve as a testing ground for new features in Chrome DevTools.
 
-
 ```sh
 pnpm install && pnpm run start
 ```
@@ -15,3 +14,60 @@ The application will now be running on `http://localhost:11224`.
 - **[React](https://react.dev/)**: Used for UI components.
 - **[Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/)**: For styling the application.
 - **[Astro DB](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/database/)**: Powers the comments feature, using a libSQL-compatible database.
+
+## Passkey UI and API integration
+
+The `/devtools-times/signin`, `/devtools-times/signup`, and
+`/devtools-times/account` pages include passkey authentication, passwordless
+registration, passkey management, and reauthentication before security changes.
+Password registration and sign-in remain available. Passkeys require HTTPS (or
+localhost) and a WebAuthn-capable browser. The browser owns the device picker,
+biometric/PIN prompts, nearby-phone flow, and security-key interaction.
+
+The client uses the existing remote API by default. Set `PUBLIC_AUTH_API_URL`
+at build time to point it at a compatible authentication backend. **This
+repository implements the UI and WebAuthn client, not the remote authentication
+backend.** The passkey/account routes below must be implemented by that backend;
+unavailable routes produce an error in the UI, never a simulated success.
+
+| Method / path                         | Request                                       | Successful response                                                                        |
+| ------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
+| `POST /login`                         | `{ username, password }`                      | JSON and an authenticated session                                                          |
+| `POST /register`                      | `{ username, password }`                      | JSON; the reader then signs in                                                             |
+| `GET /account`                        | Authenticated session                         | `{ username, hasPassword, passkeys: [{ id, name, createdAt, lastUsedAt?, deviceType? }] }` |
+| `POST /passkeys/register/options`     | `{ username, newAccount }`                    | `{ challengeId, options }`                                                                 |
+| `POST /passkeys/register/verify`      | `{ challengeId, credential, name }`           | JSON; new accounts also receive an authenticated session                                   |
+| `POST /passkeys/authenticate/options` | `{ username?, reauthenticate }`               | `{ challengeId, options }`                                                                 |
+| `POST /passkeys/authenticate/verify`  | `{ challengeId, credential, reauthenticate }` | JSON and an authenticated or recently verified session                                     |
+| `POST /reauthenticate`                | `{ password }`                                | JSON and a recently verified session                                                       |
+| `PATCH /passkeys/:id`                 | `{ name }`                                    | Updated record or `204`                                                                    |
+| `DELETE /passkeys/:id`                | None                                          | JSON or `204`                                                                              |
+| `POST /logout`                        | None                                          | JSON or `204`; invalidate the session                                                      |
+
+`options` uses the standard WebAuthn JSON representation, with base64url-encoded
+binary fields. Credentials are serialized with base64url-encoded attestation or
+assertion fields and client extension results. See the
+[WebAuthn specification](https://www.w3.org/TR/webauthn/).
+
+The backend must generate single-use, expiring challenges, bind ceremonies to
+the correct session/account/purpose, and verify the challenge, origin, RP ID,
+signature, user verification, and credential ownership. Registration should
+require discoverable credentials and user verification; reauthentication must
+require user verification. New-account creation must only finish after successful
+attestation verification. A client `newAccount` or `reauthenticate` flag must never
+substitute for server authorization. Enforce recent authentication for all
+management endpoints and reject deletion of the final sign-in method on the
+server as well as in the UI. Return `401` for an expired session and `403` for
+expired recent-auth authorization so the UI can request verification again.
+
+Requests include cookies. Configure credentialed CORS for the exact frontend
+origin and appropriate secure, HttpOnly session cookies if the API is cross-origin;
+protect all mutations against CSRF and enforce request origins on the backend.
+Do not send passwords, credentials, or session secrets to analytics or store them
+in browser storage. Error responses can provide a user-facing `message`.
+
+Build without starting a server:
+
+```sh
+pnpm run build
+```
